<div class="qul-docs">
  <p>
    A <strong>lemma</strong> is the dictionary form of a word — what you would look up in a lexicon.
      Every conjugated verb and inflected noun in the Quran maps back to one lemma, which makes it the right
      unit for counting how often a word is used.
  </p>

  <div class="docs-callout docs-callout-info">
    This resource gives the lemmas of an <strong>ayah at a time</strong>: for each ayah, the dictionary
      form of each of its words in order. If you need the lemma of one specific word instead, use the
      <a href="/resources/morphology">Word lemma</a> resource.
  </div>

  <h2>Example</h2>
  <p>Ayah <code>73:4</code> has these lemmas:</p>
  <div class="docs-code">
    <div class="docs-code-header"><span>73:4</span></div>
    <pre><code class="qpc-hafs">أَو زَادَ عَلَىٰ رَتَّلْ قُرْءَان تَرْتِيل</code></pre>
  </div>

  <h2>Export format</h2>
  <p>
    QUL exports this data as a SQLite database with one row per ayah.
  </p>
  <table>
    <thead>
      <tr>
        <th>Column</th>
        <th>Type</th>
        <th>What it holds</th>
      </tr>
    </thead>
    <tbody>
      <tr>
        <td><code>ayah_key</code></td>
        <td>TEXT</td>
        <td>The ayah, in <code>surah:ayah</code> form — for example <code>73:4</code>.</td>
      </tr>
      <tr>
        <td><code>text</code></td>
        <td>TEXT</td>
        <td>The lemma of each word in the ayah, in word order, separated by spaces.</td>
      </tr>
    </tbody>
  </table>

  <div class="docs-callout docs-callout-warning">
    The lemmas are listed in word order but are not individually numbered. To line them up with the words
    of the ayah you also need the word by word Quran script, available on the
    <a href="/resources/quran-script">Quran Scripts</a> page.
  </div>
</div>
